{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:rstar:premortem","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:756e056e4159db78d95154b46fd9a4811f797badba06a52c3fae36c2cf5a41d0","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":0.1},{"probability":0.5,"value":1.15},{"probability":0.9,"value":2.8}]},"questionHash":"sha256:20d8e05d3bea415eb4452ff8b599c74a3c3ce1a1b0c2ce11dda57a4e19490197","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq4","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"I am 70bp BELOW the Wave-1 centre (1.60/2.00/1.95) with a strong right skew. THE SHARED WAVE-1 INCONSISTENCY I AM ATTACKING: all three assign 66-80% to an AI capex impairment event (two of them argue the locked 66% is TOO LOW, at 72-80%), and all three then build a 2032-36 central case out of fiscal dominance and a WIDENING term premium - a world conditional on the bust NOT having happened. They each put the post-bust world only in their P10. That is narrative coherence mistaken for likelihood: the fiscal-dominance story is internally elegant, and it is the minority branch of their own stated probabilities. If P(bust resolved before or early in the window) = 0.70 and the post-bust path averages ~0.95%, while the no-bust fiscal-dominance path averages ~1.70% [reflecting the historical sign evidence below, not the Wave-1 2.0-2.6%], E = 0.70(0.95) + 0.30(1.70) = 0.665 + 0.510 = 1.18. THE EVIDENCE THAT BREAKS THEIR DEBT-SUPPLY ELASTICITY: Panelists 1 and 2 lean on a 2-4bp-per-1pp-of-debt/GDP elasticity. US federal debt/GDP went from ~62% (2007) to ~100% (2012) - roughly +38pp, i.e. +76 to +152bp on that elasticity - while the US 10y TIPS yield went from ~+2.4% to ~-0.7%, a 310bp FALL. Japan reached ~250% debt/GDP with a 10y real yield around zero for two decades. Over 1981-2000 US debt/GDP rose and real yields fell by ~500bp. For a reserve-currency sovereign with its own central bank, the time-series sign of the debt-supply channel is the OPPOSITE of the one the panel priced, because the fiscal deterioration and the real-yield collapse have a common cause: the bust. The panel has mistaken a cross-sectional spread story (which applies to Italy, not the G7 aggregate) for an aggregate-level one. MANDATED DECOMPOSITION, G7 GDP-weighted 2032-36 average = r* + real term premium - repression wedge. (1) r* = +0.80%. Current G7 GDP-weighted level ~0.93% on the panel's own reconstruction. I take the locked 2A language literally - \"even where a frictionless r* eases\" - and size the easing legs bigger than Wave 1 did: precautionary saving under labour-income uncertainty was sized at only -20bp by Panelist 2, when the three economies that actually ran a cognitive-employment-security shock plus aging (Japan post-1995, Korea post-2015, Germany post-2003) each show saving-rate responses consistent with -80 to -150bp on the domestic neutral rate; post-bust excess capacity suppresses investment demand (the 2002-2004 and 2009-2015 precedents) for the whole first half of the window; the Chinese surplus still recycles $500-800B/yr. Against: boomer dissaving (+25 net of the longevity leg: +5), public dissaving (+35), physical/grid capex demand (+25 raw, haircut by the bust to +10). Net change -13bp, so r* = 0.80. (2) Real term premium = +0.55%, against Wave 1's +0.75 to +1.30. Two reasons they are too high. First, SCOPE INSENSITIVITY ON THE ISSUANCE MIX: the first-line fiscal response to a rollover-cost problem is not to pay the term premium, it is to stop issuing duration. The US Treasury is already bill-heavy with a falling weighted-average maturity, and the UK DMO has been shortening since 2023. A term premium is the price of DURATION SUPPLY; shortening the mix truncates the 10-year point mechanically, and it requires no announcement, no legislation, and no \"repression\" label - which is why the panel's framing (a 40-45% probability of an announced wedge) under-counts it. Second, the largest structural duration BUYER in history arrives inside this window: OECD pension and insurance balance sheets at peak retiree-liability matching, under LDI, Solvency II matching-adjustment and IORP rules that force long bonds, which is a demographic force the panel counted only on the supply side. Offsetting upward: convenience-yield erosion, basis-trade absorption as the fragile marginal buyer (a discontinuous, not a level, risk - it belongs in the tail, not the mean), the observed synchronized long-end repricing (JGB 30y 4.235% intraday 5 Oct 2026; 30y gilts near 1998 highs). Central +0.55, 80% range 0.00 to +1.70. (3) Repression wedge = -0.40%, bigger and likelier than Wave 1's -0.25 to -0.30. All three assumed the US is \"the least likely adopter\" and GDP-weighted the wedge away. The US has the deepest precedent of any G7 sovereign: an explicit 2.5% long-bond ceiling from 1942 to 1951, Regulation Q, and a 2020 QE-plus-forward-guidance package that functioned as a cap without being called one. Repression does not require an announced cap - it is bank and money-fund liquidity rules that create captive Treasury demand, SLR calibration, Basel endgame softening, buyback and WAM management, and statutory reserve-asset requirements on new instrument classes. Several of those are already live policy in 2025-26. P(material wedge, >=50bp, somewhere in 2032-36 on >=0.80 of G7 GDP weight) = 0.60; conditional wedge ~-75bp; expected -0.40, bimodal. SUM: 0.80 + 0.55 - 0.40 = 0.95; I shade UP to 1.15 out of respect for the one hard observation that cuts against me - DFII10 at 2.60% on 30 Oct 2026 with policy TIGHTENING, and the historical fact that only ~2 of 6 G7 decade transitions since 1970 kept the average within +/-50bp of the prior level. VARIANCE ATTRIBUTION: the repression wedge carries the most variance in MY answer (bimodal, 0 vs -75 to -150bp) and therefore the left tail; the real term premium carries the second most (sigma ~0.60) and the entire right tail; r* carries the least (sigma ~0.40) and is largely orthogonal to the demographics x AI coupling, exactly as Panelist 2 argued and for his reason. I RESPECT THE 2A DISTINCTION differently from Wave 1: they satisfied the +1.0pp (r-g) deterioration through g falling to 1.7-2.05%. My CQ3 is 2.25%, so I cannot buy that route as fully as they did - I deliver the (r-g) claim through the term premium and fiscal arithmetic in the no-repression branch and concede that in my modal branch the locked +1.0pp is NOT fully reproduced, which is an explicit tension with 2A and not a smuggled reconciliation. P10 = 0.10 (bust plus heavy repression plus flight-to-quality; a 5-year average near zero real has 2011-2013 and 2020-2021 precedent), P50 = 1.15, P90 = 2.80 (fiscal dominance expressed in yields, basis-trade unwind, no repression response - the Wave-1 central case as my upper decile).","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:58.373Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"FRED DFII10 twelve-month trailing average >= 2.75% AND the NY Fed ACM 10-year nominal term premium >= 1.50%, with no new Fed purchase programme, Treasury buyback expansion, SLR recalibration or statutory captive-demand mandate announced in the interim.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2028\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Directly falsifies both my low term premium (+0.55) and my 0.60 repression probability. CQ4 P50 moves up 55-70bp to ~1.75 and the whole distribution loses its left skew; CQ6-C rises ~6pp to ~18 and CQ6-A falls ~6pp to ~27; CQ5 rises from 15 to ~26 via the MOVE conjunct. This is the Wave-1 central case being right and me being wrong for the reason they would name.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"An MSCI World peak-to-trough drawdown of >= 25% occurs AND G7 GDP-weighted 10-year nominal sovereign yields rise >= 25bp (or fail to fall 50bp) through the drawdown window.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my core CQ5 claim that a capex-bust drawdown is disinflationary and bond-bullish; my P(no rally | drawdown) of 0.28 would be revealed as the wrong template. CQ5 resolves on the second conjunct and rises to ~35-40 conditional on the MOVE leg; CQ4's P90 fattens toward 3.2 and its P50 up ~30bp; CQ6-C up ~5pp, CQ6-D down ~5pp.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Realized PPP-weighted real GDP growth for OECD-38 + China over 2027-2031 (IMF WEO / OECD Economic Outlook, constant-PPP aggregation) comes in at or below 1.60%/yr, with China's own 2029-2031 average below 3.0%.\",\"by_when\":\"Jun 30, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my double-subtraction correction - it would mean the locked 2A drag coefficients ARE counterfactual-net and Wave 1 was right to subtract them whole. CQ3 P50 falls from 2.25 to ~1.75 and the right tail compresses to P90 ~2.8; CQ6-A collapses from 33 to ~16, CQ6-D rises from 17 to ~24, CQ6-B back toward ~47; hypothesis plank (d) falls from 22 to ~12.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"IEA / EIA reported strict operational data-centre electricity consumption across OECD-38 + China at or below 650 TWh/yr, with publicly identified cancelled-or-impaired announced capacity above 25% of the 2025-2027 announcement book.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my central coupling finding that impairment is a capital-structure event rather than a kilowatt-hour event (the 1999-2002 fibre precedent, where capacity was financially written off and then fully utilized by ~2010). It would vindicate the unanimous Wave-1 direction on AI-Q3. My CQ3 TFP term drops ~0.15pp (P50 to ~2.10), CQ6-A down ~5pp, CQ6-D up ~5pp, and my upward revision of AI-Q1 is withdrawn.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China headline CPI 36-month trailing average below 0.80% with core CPI below 1.0% and PPI back below zero y/y (NBS monthly releases).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my 0.48 marginal on CQ2's China conjunct and vindicates the Wave-1 Japan-template reading at 0.70-0.76. CQ2 rises from 16 to ~25-27. It would also raise CQ6-D by ~4pp (China exporting goods deflation into a demand-shortfall OECD) and lower my OECD core central by ~15bp, pulling CQ6-C down ~3pp.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run2 wave 2 premortem seat, effort 'rstar'."}}